capture: readiness polling
runtime: Java 17
commit: migration change set, a verified successor of base commit c8f6226105.
  No hash is recorded here, and none is invented: the revision that carries
  this file cannot be written inside the file it commits.  The relationship
  was measured rather than assumed - git merge-base --is-ancestor reports that
  the base commit is an ancestor of this working tree's head - so the
  descriptor names the revision precisely without fabricating a value for it.
source: HTTP polling of the deployed application
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no six-service reference stack and no deployed web archive on a
  servlet container, therefore no running application to poll and no readiness
  sequence to record.  Measured on this host rather than assumed, and every
  figure that follows is an observation.  The hostname arkcase-ce.local does
  not resolve.  The readiness target https://arkcase-ce.local/arkcase/ and all
  four other documented service URLs observed HTTP status 000, which is this
  tree's token for no HTTP response at all, with transport exit status 6 and
  the diagnostic "Could not resolve host: arkcase-ce.local".  That same
  diagnostic is returned when certificate verification is disabled, which
  proves the failure is name resolution and not certificate verification, so
  the self-signed certificate recorded below never even entered the picture.
  TOMCAT_HOME, CATALINA_HOME, CATALINA_BASE and CATALINA_LOG are all unset, no
  container launcher is on PATH, and no servlet container is installed in any
  of the usual locations.  No web archive of any kind exists anywhere in the
  working tree.  This region cannot be captured without a live deployment.
  The migrated runtime is deliberately NOT listed among the unmet
  preconditions, because it is not one: Java 17 is installed here and answers
  17.0.19, and the sibling env/toolchain.txt records that same banner for this
  side of the comparison.  What is missing is the deployment and its backing
  services, not the JVM.  The counterpart region recorded the mirror-image
  finding for the older runtime, so the two sides are absent for the same
  reason rather than for two different ones, and the delta between them is the
  runtime label alone.
normalised: nothing, because no poll record was captured.  The policy is
  stated in full anyway, so that this side reproduces the transformation
  rather than guesses at it, and so that a later run has no latitude to invent
  one.  When this region is captured, the pipeline strips timestamps and
  dates, the Date response header, Set-Cookie values, entity tags, session
  identifiers, generated request and instance identifiers, absolute filesystem
  paths in favour of a stable token, port assignments, elapsed-time and
  duration figures, and retry back-off values.  It preserves the
  behaviour-bearing content: the attempt ordinal of every attempt, the
  observed HTTP status code of every attempt, the target path, the readiness
  verdict, and any response-body marker that evidences readiness.  The shape
  of the sequence - how many attempts it took, and which statuses preceded the
  ready one - IS the evidence this region exists to carry, so over-normalising
  it would destroy that evidence rather than tidy it, and that is the
  dangerous failure rather than the visible one, because it makes two captures
  agree for a reason unrelated to behaviour.
redacted: nothing, because no poll record was captured.  When it is captured,
  every credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder
  <REDACTED>, which is the convention this documentation tree already uses.
  The scan that finds them looks for password=, passwd, user=, secret and
  token=, and for Authorization and Cookie header values, and every matched
  value is expected to read <REDACTED> afterwards.  Three carriers are in
  scope.  The documented administrator credential, which is never transcribed
  here in any form, not even as an illustration of what redaction removes.  An
  Authorization header, or a userinfo component inside a probed URL, either of
  which would appear if an authenticated probe were ever added to this region;
  readiness polling requires no credential and the producer's readiness probe
  is anonymous by design, so neither is expected, and if one ever appeared the
  credential would be redacted and the fact stated in this field.  And
  keystore or trust-store passwords, which a poll that echoed its transport
  configuration would emit, and which are recorded only in the redacted forms
  keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>.  Session
  cookie values are handled by the normalisation policy above rather than
  here, because they are stripped outright instead of placeholdered.  A fourth
  carrier is handled by description instead of replacement, and is set out in
  the notes below.
