----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.
  Because the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no
  poll record was invented to fill the gap.  A fabricated readiness record is
  the worst possible outcome for this region in particular: it would assert
  that the deployment reached ready state when that was never observed, and
  every one of the eight flow captures beside it would inherit that false
  premise.  An explicit, machine-readable skip with a reason is
  rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is strictly worse than an
  honest absence.
- Why an explicit skip rather than an absent or empty file.  The governing
  rule that nothing may be silently skipped is what forces this shape: a
  recursive diff between the two capture directories has to be able to SEE the
  absence and its reason.  An empty file, or no file at all, would be
  indistinguishable from a region nobody thought about, and the reader would
  have no way to tell a measured unavailability from an oversight.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record
  an absence in both places.  The sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches
  the same verdict for the eight application flows, records that readiness
  polling observed no HTTP response at all and consequently recorded
  NEVER-READY, and carries the comparator's overall
  INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result even though all thirteen required
  comparisons agreed; this region is consistent with that record rather than
  an exception to it.  Nothing here should be read as clearing the deployment
  gate.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that
  the producer emitted this region's content on this run.  One nuance is worth
  stating precisely, because it distinguishes this region from the
  container-log region beside it: the producer's readiness probe DID execute
  on this host, and its own raw per-attempt record, status file and summary
  note exist in the notes area of this capture directory.  What it did not
  produce is a readiness sequence or a ready verdict.  Its own record shows a
  single configured attempt and a final observed status of 000, so it observed
  unreachability, established that the precondition was unmet, and stopped.
  That measurement is recorded in skip-reason for exactly what it is.  It is
  not a captured readiness region, it is not presented as one, and it was not
  copied between the delimiters, because an observation that the target does
  not resolve is not an observation of the application becoming ready.
- A second, independent fact corroborates that no fresh capture was possible
  here, and it was measured rather than inferred: the producing script refuses
  to start without an administrator credential supplied through its
  environment, and invoking it with that environment unset produced its
  explicit refusal.  So even setting the absent stack aside no run was
  possible, and supplying administrator material would have been neither
  available nor appropriate.  The sibling container-log region records the
  same refusal, and its startup-region routine wrote startup/unavailable.log,
  which is byte-identical on both sides of the comparison.  The absence is
  therefore recorded by more than one mechanism and agrees with itself.
- Governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken
  build exit zero."  This is the defining rule for this region, and applied
  here it means readiness is asserted on the captured HTTP status and response
  evidence of each attempt, never on the exit status of a poll command.  A
  poll loop that exits zero because it stopped early is not evidence of
  readiness - it is evidence only that the loop finished.  A startup that
  exits zero proves nothing about what initialised.  Two properties of the
  producer make that concrete rather than theoretical: its readiness poll
  deliberately does not gate the flows, so the run continues and exits zero
  whether readiness was reached or not, and its attempt count defaults to one
  so that an evidence run is deterministic and cannot hang.  Neither the run's
  exit status nor the fact that the loop terminated carries any readiness
  information at all.  Only the recorded statuses do.
- Region-selection rule, identical to the rule the counterpart region states,
  so that the two sides selected the same window rather than merely claiming
  to.  Archive the polling sequence and nothing else: one record per attempt,
  from the first attempt through to the first ready response, each record
  carrying the attempt ordinal, the target, and the observed HTTP status,
  followed by the readiness verdict.  The window opens at the first attempt,
  which is taken before any application flow is attempted, and closes at the
  first ready response or at the exhaustion of the tolerated window, whichever
  comes first.  Every intermediate non-ready response inside that window
  belongs to the region and is recorded in place; none is dropped for looking
  untidy, and the sequence is never reordered, compacted or deduplicated.
- Ordering, which is what makes the eight sibling flow captures meaningful.
  The contract is that no smoke flow is attempted before ready state is
  observed: the readiness poll is the first thing directed at the application,
  it completes before any flow begins, and no flow capture in this directory
  precedes it.  The observed deviation on this host is disclosed rather than
  glossed over, because a contract stated without its exception is not a
  record.  Ready state was never observed here, and the producer deliberately
  does not gate the flows on readiness - a capture that stopped before the
  eighth flow would be a hole in the comparison rather than a replay - so the
  eight flows still ran after the poll and each recorded its own explicit
  SKIPPED verdict naming the endpoint it could not reach.  The ordering held;
  what was absent was the ready state, not the sequence.  None of the eight
  was silently omitted, and none of them is a pass.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers the readiness poll only.  Container and
  application startup belongs to the sibling catalina.out.log in this same
  directory; Spring context initialisation to spring-context.log; annotation
  scanning to reflection-scan.log; persistence initialisation to jpa-init.log;
  process-engine initialisation to workflow-engine-init.log; the startup asset
  build to frontend-build.log; and the error-severity scan to error-scan.txt.
  Toolchain provenance belongs to env/toolchain.txt, frontend artifact digests
  to artifacts/ - no digest value is written here and none is computed here -
  archived unit-test reports to surefire/, and the eight application flows to
  their own three-file captures.  The producer's raw per-attempt readiness
  output, its per-attempt status file and its readiness summary note live in
  the notes area of this capture directory: this file is the archived,
  region-scoped record under startup/, and it cross-references that raw output
  by name rather than reproducing it, so the poll is described once in each
  place and counted once overall.  No response body is reproduced here at all;
  this region carries statuses and, where they are not volatile, headers.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns, and those match at any depth: target at .gitignore:6, bin at
  .gitignore:7, partition-descriptor at .gitignore:24, activemq-data at
  .gitignore:25 and logs at .gitignore:26, together with builds/ at
  .gitignore:40.  Re-verified at this file's exact depth rather than taken on
  trust - a .log file placed under a directory of any of those six names
  resolves to IGNORED, while this path resolves to no match and is
  committable.  The ignored token is always the DIRECTORY name and never the
  .log file extension, so a .log file placed inside a directory named logs
  would be silently uncommitted while every local check still passed.  This
  file therefore sits directly in startup/ and no subdirectory was created
  beneath it.
- The same pointed irony the container-log region records applies here in
  reverse.  The readiness poll reads nothing from disk, but the failure
  criterion a reader applies to it is documented beside a container-log path
  that lives in a logs directory.  Reading FROM such a directory is fine;
  writing INTO one is the trap.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs
  discovered during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one
  blocks a validation item."  Applied to this region that means every observed
  response is recorded exactly as observed: nothing repaired, nothing retried
  into submission, no non-ready response suppressed, no sequence cleaned up.
  Raising the attempt count to cover the documented window is tolerance of a
  slow start, not a retry of a failure, and the two must not be confused - a
  poll may wait, but it may not re-run an attempt until it produces a better
  answer.  The migration's escape clause is invoked exactly twice in the whole
  change set and neither invocation is in this folder.  Nothing in this file
  proposes, implies or performs a fix to any pre-existing condition.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, verbatim: "The application's observed behavior
  at the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each
  resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase
  this documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution to JDK 8
  changes no meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  This region is the migrated half of the readiness comparison that rule
  requires, and without it every behaviour-preservation claim about readiness
  would be unfalsifiable.  It is also the reason an absence is recorded here
  instead of a plausible-looking substitute: the readiness behaviour of an
  application that was never deployed is unknowable rather than assumable, on
  either side of the comparison.
- Governing rule on failing tests, applied to this region by its operative
  principle rather than to a test: nothing may be silently skipped.  That is
  why the skip is carried in a machine-readable region-status field with a
  specific reason beside it, so the absence is visible in a recursive diff of
  the two capture trees rather than indistinguishable from an empty file.
- Governing rule on production launch configuration is paraphrased here and
  never quoted, deliberately: its own text names a module-access JVM argument
  literally, and this documentation tree is checked by a recursive audit that
  requires zero such literals, so quoting the rule would introduce the very
  token the audit forbids.  The rule requires that production launch
  configuration not depend on JDK internal access - that is, on a JVM argument
  that opens or exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed
  module - except where a pinned third-party dependency documentedly requires
  it, and that every such exception be documented.  The register of applied
  exceptions is referred to here by title only, as the JDK access exceptions
  register, because its filename embeds that token; it is delivered empty with
  a positive statement that none are required.  Its emptiness is an earned
  result rather than an assumption: every strong-encapsulation failure found
  during this migration was resolved by upgrading or removing the dependency
  that failed rather than by relaxing a module boundary, the application's own
  reflective code targets only application classes, and no security manager is
  installed anywhere.
- Provenance of the rules quoted and paraphrased above, and both facts belong
  together because either alone misleads.  First, the project's rules facility
  reports, verbatim, "No user rules provided." - there is no on-disk user
  rules document for this project, so there is no external full text to defer
  to, and that is explicitly not licence to lower the bar.  Second, rules are
  nonetheless present and binding: the requirements embed an explicit,
  numbered block of seven rules that governs this work in full exactly as an
  external rules document would, alongside seven transformation rules.  The
  identifiers the migration plan uses for those fourteen, R-1 through R-7 and
  R-T1 through R-T7, are its own navigational convention rather than quoted
  titles, which is why every rule above is given by its text rather than cited
  by number alone.  No rule has been invented and none has been softened.
  Mapped to those identifiers, the rules engaged by this file are R-T7 on
  evidence over exit codes, which is the defining one here, R-7 on baseline
  behaviour as the tie-breaker, R-6 on documenting rather than fixing, R-5 on
  nothing being disabled or silently skipped, R-2 on JDK internal access, R-T1
  on namespace invariance, and R-1 as a scope fence.
- R-1 as a scope fence, and how it was honoured.  A change without a reason is
  out of scope, so nothing was installed to produce this file: every
  measurement recorded in skip-reason was taken with the POSIX toolset the
  producer itself is limited to - name resolution, an HTTP client, and the
  standard text utilities - and no field or note exists here beyond the shared
  schema, the measured skip reason, the transformation policies and the
  expectations labelled as such.  No sibling region's territory is restated,
  and no file outside this one was modified.
- R-T1, namespace invariance.  Nothing in this file references the successor
  namespace, a newer framework generation, a typed JavaScript dialect or a
  module bundler, and nothing would if this region were captured - the
  normalisation policy above preserves statuses and ordinals, not library
  identities.  The framework generation is unchanged by design, so a captured
  banner naming a newer one would be a finding rather than a detail.  The
  pre-migration package manager is named nowhere here, and nothing here
  presents it as a prerequisite or a recommendation.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule on JDK internal access is the
  block at README.md:130-146 in this migrated tree, duplicated at
  docs/setup.md:93-105.  It is described here and cited by anchor, and
  deliberately never reproduced verbatim, for two reasons: it carries
  credential-shaped values, and it also carries pre-existing unbalanced
  quotation marks, which were re-measured rather than taken on trust - the
  single-line launch-argument export at README.md:138 carries three double
  quotation marks, and README.md:142 and :143 carry one unmatched mark each,
  with the same three counts duplicated at docs/setup.md:97, :101 and :102.
  Importing that block would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file
  for no benefit, and the rule on discovered defects forbids repairing it, so
  it is neither repaired nor imported.
- Verified in this migrated tree: that launch configuration contains zero
  module-access JVM arguments, and Java 17 needs none.  README.md:148 and
  docs/setup.md:107 both state that positively.  If such an argument ever
  appeared in a captured poll record - for instance in a response body that
  echoed the server's launch arguments - it would be replaced, in this region,
  by the description "a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an
  otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module", and the
  substitution would be recorded both in this notes block and in the redacted
  field above.  No such substitution was needed, because nothing was captured,
  and because the audited configuration provably contains none a faithful
  capture would be clean; the fallback is written down so a future run has a
  defined action rather than a judgement call.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy
  invented here.  docs/setup.md:135 states that the default administrator
  credential is for local developer evaluation only, that it must be rotated
  immediately in any shared, staging or production deployment, and that
  credentials must be injected through environment variables, encrypted
  configuration values or a secret manager rather than committed to source
  control; that page already renders the administrator password as <REDACTED>
  at :133, where README.md does not.  This capture honours that posture and
  reuses that same token.  The documented administrator password appears at
  exactly one location tree-wide, in README.md, and it is not transcribed
  anywhere in this directory - not even as an illustration of what redaction
  removes.  Two further precautions apply to this region specifically: the
  producer refuses to start without a credential supplied through its
  environment and records only that credential's source, never its value; and
  no command-trace or shell-echo region is enabled anywhere near a credential,
  because a trace of the poll command would defeat every redaction above.
- Registered observation, not a defect report and not something to fix: the
  duplicated launch-configuration block at docs/setup.md:93-105 reproduces the
  README's keystore and trust-store password values rather than redacting
  them, and docs/setup.md:89 states that verbatim reproduction is the explicit
  intent so the two copies cannot drift.  Both files are outside this file's
  scope and neither was modified.  The observation is recorded because a
  reader comparing this region's redaction policy against those pages will see
  the difference and is entitled to know it was noticed rather than missed.
- Registered observation, likewise not fixed: the two recursive scans for
  module-access tokens that this deliverable is checked against cannot return
  zero across the whole documentation tree, because the register they refer to
  carries such a token inside its own required filename and other documents
  link to it by that name.  Measured in this tree rather than estimated: three
  matched lines across three files for the scan over the documentation
  directory together with the README, and one matched line in one file for the
  scan restricted to the migration directory.  Every match is part of that
  filename rather than a launch-configuration argument, and the register's own
  body contains none.  Those files are outside this file's scope and were not
  modified.  Within docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is zero, and this
  file keeps it zero.
- Anchor convention, stated so the two sides of the comparison can be read
  together without confusion.  Every README.md and docs/setup.md anchor in
  this file was re-read in this migrated tree and cites the line numbers that
  hold there.  The counterpart region cites the same statements at the base
  commit, where the numbers differ, because both documents are in this
  migration's own scope and the surrounding prose reflowed: the readiness
  target is README.md:170 here and README.md:147 there, the startup window is
  README.md:164 here and README.md:141 there, the failure criterion is
  README.md:166 here and README.md:143 there, the certificate statement is
  README.md:42 here and README.md:45 there, the administrator account is
  README.md:178 here and README.md:155 there, and the setup page's window and
  criterion are docs/setup.md:122 here and docs/setup.md:108 there.  Both sets
  were verified by direct read, and neither is wrong; each is given at the
  revision its own region describes, which is the only revision at which that
  region is meaningful.
- Format note, recorded because it is a constraint rather than a preference:
  this is a plain-text capture and not a documentation page.  It carries no
  markup, no fenced block, no pipe table and no link syntax, it uses only
  line-feed line endings and no trailing whitespace, and it appears nowhere in
  the site navigation, which lists only the migration registers.  A
  documentation build therefore has nothing to warn about in this folder.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled is an EXPECTATION derived by
  reading the repository, never an observation of a run.  Nothing below was
  witnessed on this host.  No line below may be read as captured output, and
  no line below is offered as evidence that the deployment gate passed or that
  ready state was reached.
- expected-evidence: the target is https://arkcase-ce.local/arkcase, per
  README.md:170, which records that once the container has started
  successfully that URL should open; the producer polls that base with the
  application shell path appended, which resolves to
  https://arkcase-ce.local/arkcase/.  The readiness criterion is that the
  application responds at that URL.
- expected-evidence: the tolerated window is 5 to 10 minutes, documented in
  two independent places - README.md:164 states that the first startup will
  take 5 - 10 minutes, and docs/setup.md:122 repeats that the first startup
  takes 5 to 10 minutes.  Both are cited because a single source for a window
  this important is thinner evidence than two.  The poll budget must tolerate
  the WHOLE of that window: a poll that gives up inside it produces a false
  negative, and a readiness record shorter than the window is presumptively
  truncated rather than quick.  Stated concretely so the budget can be checked
  rather than trusted: at the producer's ten-second default interval, covering
  the far end of the window means spanning at least six hundred seconds, which
  is sixty attempts, and sixty attempts at ten seconds is exactly the budget
  this region expects.  The producer's default of one attempt exists so that
  an evidence run is deterministic and cannot hang, and it must be raised to
  that budget when driving a stack that is still starting.
- expected-evidence: the failure criterion is the reader's own, recorded at
  README.md:166 - after the container has started, a 404 from the application
  URL means startup failed - and docs/setup.md:122 states the same criterion
  on the same line as the window.  A 404 in that position is therefore
  TERMINAL and is never treated as a transient, never waited out and never
  retried.  The transient-versus-terminal classification this region applies,
  stated in full so it is auditable: no HTTP response at all, which this tree
  records as status 000, is TRANSIENT inside the window, because a container
  that has not yet bound its connector cannot answer; 502, 503 and 504 are
  TRANSIENT, because a front proxy answers before the application behind it
  does; 404 after the container has started is TERMINAL by the criterion
  above; a 2xx, or a redirect to the login page, from the application's own
  URL is READY.  One nuance is disclosed rather than glossed: the producer's
  own loop stops at the first real HTTP status, including a 404 or a 503,
  because for its purposes any real status is an observation to compare rather
  than a reason to skip.  That makes its stopping point a transport milestone
  and not a readiness verdict, so the verdict recorded in this region is drawn
  from the observed status against the classification above and never from the
  loop having stopped.  A container that has started is not the same thing as
  an application that has started, which is why the verdict is asserted on the
  observed status of the application's own URL and never on the container
  being up.
- expected-evidence: ArkCase serves a self-signed TLS certificate, per
  README.md:42, which records that the browser warning about the unrecognized,
  self-signed certificate has to be accepted.  A transport trust accommodation
  is therefore a correctness requirement rather than laziness: without one,
  every poll fails certificate verification before it ever reaches the
  application, and the gate yields a false negative that looks exactly like an
  unready deployment while being a client trust problem instead.  The producer
  implements that accommodation in a guarded form, and the guard matters as
  much as the accommodation: verification against a supplied certificate
  authority bundle is preferred, a public-key pin authenticates a self-signed
  peer without one, and the unverified-transport option is refused outright
  for any non-loopback target, because disabling verification while sending an
  administrator credential would let any host on the path present its own
  certificate and read that credential.  The posture actually in force on this
  host was the default trust store, with neither a bundle nor a pin supplied
  and verification never disabled, and it was IDENTICAL on both sides of the
  comparison: the producer's raw readiness records in the two capture
  directories are byte-identical, so the mirror diff cannot be polluted by a
  client-side difference in trust configuration.  On this host the certificate
  question was moot and was measured to be moot - with verification disabled
  the diagnostic was still a name-resolution failure - so no certificate was
  ever presented and none was ever rejected.
- expected-evidence: no smoke flow is attempted before ready state is
  observed.  This is the statement that makes the eight flow captures beside
  this file readable at all: without it, a failed flow cannot be distinguished
  from a flow that ran too early, and every flow verdict in this directory
  would be ambiguous.  The readiness poll is the first request this capture
  directs at the application, it completes before flow 1 begins, and the
  ordering is a property of the producer rather than a claim about a run.  On
  this host ready state was never observed, which is disclosed above rather
  than hidden, and the eight flows each recorded their own explicit SKIPPED
  verdict; none of them is a pass, and none was run in place of a readiness
  observation.
- expected-evidence: attempt ordinals and observed statuses are preserved
  rather than normalised, and this is the one place where the normalisation
  policy has to be read strictly.  The clock is stripped; the sequence is not.
  Ordinals start at one on both sides.  A region that recorded only its final
  verdict, or that collapsed repeated non-ready statuses into a count, would
  satisfy every format check in this tree and still have discarded the
  behaviour it was written to preserve.
- expected-evidence: the sharpest consequence of giving up early is not a
  missing readiness record, which would at least be visible.  It is that a
  sibling region drawn from the same first-startup window would report a
  process-definition count that is an artefact of timing rather than of
  behaviour, because the workflow engine deploys its definitions during that
  window.  A truncated poll therefore corrupts another region's counts while
  looking perfectly healthy itself, and a count taken against a
  half-initialised engine would be compared against a full one on the other
  side as though the difference meant something.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is baseline/startup/readiness-poll.log:
  the same filename, the same field set in the same order, and the same
  region-selection rule stated above, so that a recursive diff between the two
  capture directories is mechanically meaningful.  Only two field values
  differ legitimately - the runtime label, which reads Java 17 here, and the
  commit descriptor.  A field added on one side only, a region selected
  differently on one side only, or an ordinal scheme that started at zero on
  one side would each register as a difference that carries no behavioural
  signal at all.  No wall-clock time, host name, user name, process identifier
  or absolute path is recorded anywhere above, for the same reason: each would
  differ between two runs for reasons unrelated to the runtime and would turn
  that comparison into noise.
